# Cloudflare Pages reads this file and attaches these response headers to
# matching paths. It is not a dashboard setting - it lives in git, deploys with
# the site, and is reviewable in a diff. (Netlify uses the same format.)
#
# Rules are matched top to bottom; a path can pick up headers from more than
# one block.

# Keep the whole site out of search results.
#
# The <meta name="robots" content="noindex"> in index.html only covers HTML.
# A JPEG has no HTML, so there is nowhere to put a meta tag - and Google
# Images indexes image URLs directly, independently of the page they appear
# on. An HTTP header is the only way to reach them. This matters little today
# and a great deal once the guest photos are online.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# Images and video change by getting a new filename rather than by being
# overwritten - see the og:image note in index.html for why. That makes them
# safe to cache hard. 30 days rather than a year so that if someone does
# overwrite one, the mistake heals itself in a month instead of outliving
# everyone's patience.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/video/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
